  <p>
    When we see astronauts floating on the International Space Station, we often call it "zero gravity." But
    that's actually a misnomer. The ISS experiences about 90% of Earth's surface gravity. So why do astronauts
    float? The answer reveals a fascinating truth about gravity and orbital mechanics.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Microgravity?</h2>
  <p>
    Microgravity refers to conditions where gravitational forces are present but their effects are greatly reduced.
    In orbit, objects are in continuous free fall around Earth, creating the sensation and effects of weightlessness
    even though gravity is still pulling on them.
  </p>

  <h3>The Orbital Free Fall</h3>
  <p>
    Imagine throwing a ball horizontally. It follows a curved path as gravity pulls it down while it moves forward.
    Now imagine throwing it so fast that as it falls, Earth's surface curves away beneath it at the same rate. The
    ball would be in orbit—constantly falling but never hitting the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>True Zero Gravity</h2>
  <p>
    True zero gravity would only exist infinitely far from all masses, where gravitational fields become essentially
    zero. Since the universe is full of massive objects, there's no place with literally zero gravitational force.
    Even in intergalactic space, faint gravitational influences exist.
  </p>

  <h3>Why the Term Persists</h3>
  <p>
    We continue using "zero gravity" colloquially because it accurately describes the experience—weightlessness—
    even if it's not technically accurate. "Microgravity" is the preferred scientific term, acknowledging that
    small gravitational effects remain.
  </p>

  <h2>Experiencing Weightlessness on Earth</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need to go to space to experience microgravity briefly. Any time you're in free fall, you're
    weightless:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The brief moment at the top of a jump</li>
    <li>Riding a drop tower at an amusement park</li>
    <li>Flying in a parabolic arc in an airplane (the "Vomit Comet")</li>
    <li>Skydiving before air resistance builds up</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Effects of Microgravity</h2>

  <h3>On the Human Body</h3>
  <p>
    Extended periods in microgravity cause significant physiological changes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Bone density decreases at about 1-2% per month</li>
    <li>Muscles atrophy without resistance to work against</li>
    <li>Fluids shift toward the head, causing "puffy face"</li>
    <li>The cardiovascular system doesn't need to work as hard</li>
    <li>The immune system becomes suppressed</li>
    <li>Vision can be affected by intracranial pressure changes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>On Physics and Chemistry</h3>
  <p>
    Microgravity enables unique scientific research:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Flames burn spherically instead of teardrop-shaped</li>
    <li>Convection doesn't occur, affecting mixing and crystal growth</li>
    <li>Liquids form perfect spheres due to surface tension</li>
    <li>Protein crystals can grow larger and more perfect</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Creating Artificial Gravity</h2>
  <p>
    For long-duration space missions, artificial gravity might be necessary to maintain crew health. The most
    practical approach is rotation: spinning a spacecraft or habitat creates centrifugal force that mimics
    gravity.
  </p>

  <h3>The Engineering Challenge</h3>
  <p>
    Creating comfortable artificial gravity requires large structures rotating at just the right speed. Too small
    or too fast causes disorienting Coriolis effects. This remains a major engineering challenge for future
    long-duration missions to Mars or beyond.
  </p>

  <h2>Life in Microgravity</h2>
  <p>
    Astronauts adapt to microgravity but face daily challenges:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Everything must be secured or it floats away</li>
    <li>Sleeping requires strapping into a sleeping bag</li>
    <li>Eating and drinking require special techniques</li>
    <li>Exercise requires elastic bands or specialized equipment</li>
    <li>Using the bathroom is complicated and requires suction</li>
  </ul>

